Under the characteristic and consistency hypotheses, if an accumulator is diagonally fixed, then one gated step on any entry remains diagonally fixed. A successful gate passes to the intersection, which stays in the fixed neighbourhood; a failed gate leaves the accumulator unchanged.

Unfold the step. If the characteristic of the entry is not one, the product of the one-tests is zero, selection returns the accumulator, and the hypothesis applies. If it is one, decide the consistency test of the accumulator with the second component of the entry. Failure again returns the accumulator. Success returns the intersection index. The second component is diagonally fixed by the characteristic hypothesis, recovering the pair by pairing after unpairing. Consistency supplies a witness of the intersection inclusion. The intersection is a neighbourhood because the intersection specification rewrites it to a presented neighbourhood. It lies in the fixed neighbourhood by intersection closure of the fixed-neighbourhood subsystem. Diagonal fixedness of the intersection index follows by the fixed-neighbourhood characterization and the intersection specification.
